"gasStove","v":"grate","w":"grate"},{"p":"gasStove","v":"pot","w":"pot"},{"p":"gasStove","v":"water","w":"water","d":1.5,"o":-0.7}],"shots":[{"focus":["gasStove"],"width":0.85}]},{"s":"congrats","do":[],"shots":[{"focus":"home"}]}],"audio":{"cards":[{"text":"1,950 °C","at":{"s":"fact","w":"1,950"},"d":1.8}],"sfx":[{"lib":"water_rush","at":{"s":"problem","w":"hisses"},"d":1.2},{"lib":"flame","at":{"s":"fix1","w":"fire"},"d":1.4},{"lib":"flame","at":{"s":"fix2","w":"mixes"},"d":1.2},{"lib":"water_rush","at":{"s":"finish","w":"water"},"d":1}],"chimes":[{"s":"name"},{"s":"fact","w":"1,950"},{"s":"congrats"}]},"codeShots":true,"why":"A gas stove in a kitchen cutaway: a custom rig shows the gas pipe, valve and knob, jet, mixing tube, air shutter and burner ring, with the flame and gas dots running through it. The camera starts mid on the stove, widens to a box shot to catch the leaked gas and soot rising, then goes close on the burner head for the name, the mixing tube for the how, and home for the finish. The chain runs from raw gas leaking, to a spark that lights a lazy yellow sooty flame, to an air shutter that turns the flame blue and hot. The myth shows the yellow flame, then takes it away.","hold":true},"sentences":[{"role":"hook","text":"If you open a gas pipe to a metal ring, you have a stove."},{"role":"problem","text":"But the gas hisses out of the ring, and it has no smell on its own."},{"role":"fix1","text":"So you add a spark, and the gas catches fire as a flame."},{"role":"problem2","text":"But the flame is lazy and yellow, and it throws off black soot."},{"role":"fix2","text":"So you add a shutter that mixes air into the gas, and the flame turns blue."},{"role":"name","text":"This is a gas burner."},{"role":"how","text":"Gas shoots through a tiny jet and drags air into the tube, so it burns clean."},{"role":"fact","text":"A blue flame reaches a theoretical maximum of about 1,950 °C."},{"role":"myth","text":"And a big yellow flame is not hotter, though it looks it."},{"role":"mythFact","text":"Only the blue flame gets enough air to burn the gas, so it is hotter."},{"role":"finish","text":"Add a grate that holds a pot of water over the flame."},{"role":"congrats","text":"Congratulations, you have built a gas stove."}]}